@article{aimeur_explain_2025,
 abstract = {This study investigates the application of machine learning techniques for predicting the mechanical properties of friction stir welded aluminum alloys. Friction stir welding (FSW) is a solid-state joining process widely used in aerospace and automotive industries due to its advantages over conventional fusion welding. However, optimizing process parameters such as rotational speed, traverse speed, and tool pin profile remains challenging. In this work, we employ the Taguchi method combined with artificial neural networks (ANN) and support vector regression (SVR) to model and predict tensile strength and hardness of AA6061-T6 welds. Experimental data from L27 orthogonal array design were used to train and validate the models. Results show that ANN outperforms SVR with a mean absolute error of less than 5% for tensile strength prediction. The optimal parameters identified are 1000 rpm rotational speed, 60 mm/min traverse speed, and square pin profile, achieving a 15% improvement in joint efficiency. Sensitivity analysis reveals rotational speed as the most influential factor. This hybrid approach provides a robust framework for parameter optimization in FSW, reducing experimental trials and enhancing weld quality.},
