@article{artsi_large_2025,
 abstract = {Background: Artificial intelligence (AI) has shown great promise in enhancing diagnostic accuracy in medical imaging. However, its integration into clinical workflows remains challenging due to issues of interpretability and trust.

Methods: This study develops a novel explainable AI (XAI) framework for chest X-ray analysis, incorporating attention mechanisms and counterfactual explanations. We evaluated the model on a dataset of 10,000 images from public repositories, comparing performance against black-box models using metrics such as AUC-ROC, sensitivity, and specificity. User studies with 50 radiologists assessed trust and usability.

Results: The XAI model achieved an AUC-ROC of 0.95, outperforming standard CNNs (0.92) while providing interpretable heatmaps that aligned with expert annotations in 85% of cases. Radiologists reported 30% higher trust scores compared to non-explainable models.

Conclusion: The proposed XAI framework bridges the gap between AI performance and clinical adoption, offering a scalable solution for informatics in medicine.},
