@inproceedings{ashaduzzaman_leveraging_2025,
 abstract = {Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) tools have recently gained significant traction in software engineering (SE), promising to automate and enhance various aspects of the SE lifecycle. However, the rapid adoption of GenAI in SE raises important questions about its implications for developers, teams, and organizations. In this paper, we present a comprehensive survey of GenAI tools and practices in SE. We systematically review 50+ GenAI tools across key SE phases, including requirements engineering, design, implementation, testing, and maintenance. Our analysis reveals that while GenAI excels in code generation and bug detection, challenges persist in areas like ethical considerations, integration with existing workflows, and long-term reliability. We also identify emerging trends, such as the use of GenAI for collaborative coding and automated documentation. Through qualitative insights from 20 industry practitioners, we discuss adoption barriers, best practices, and future directions. Our findings provide a foundational understanding for researchers and practitioners navigating the integration of GenAI in SE.},
