@article{barrit_specialized_2025,
 abstract = {Background Ventral brainstem cavernous malformations (CMs) are challenging lesions due to their deep location and proximity to critical neural structures. The endoscopic endonasal approach (EEA) has emerged as a viable option for selected cases. Methods We reviewed our institutional experience with EEA for ventral brainstem CMs. Patient demographics, clinical presentation, surgical technique, outcomes, and complications were analyzed. Results Five patients underwent EEA for ventral brainstem CMs between 2015 and 2023. All lesions were located in the pons or midbrain. Complete resection was achieved in four cases. One patient had a subtotal resection due to adherence to the basilar artery. Neurological improvement was observed in all patients postoperatively. No major complications occurred. Conclusions EEA is a safe and effective approach for selected ventral brainstem CMs, offering direct access and minimal brain retraction.},
