@incollection{beale_large_2025,
 abstract = {Mobile app developers frequently reuse user interface (UI) designs from existing apps to accelerate development and ensure familiarity for users. However, the extent, patterns, and implications of such reuse remain underexplored. In this paper, we present a large-scale empirical study of UI design reuse in Android apps. We develop a novel pipeline to detect and quantify UI similarities across 1.2 million app screens from 150,000 apps collected over eight years. Our analysis reveals that UI reuse is pervasive: over 70% of screens exhibit significant similarity to at least one other screen, with popular design patterns (e.g., login forms, navigation drawers) being cloned hundreds of thousands of times. We identify temporal trends, showing increasing reuse over time, and spatial patterns, such as clustering around app stores and developer communities. Furthermore, we uncover risks including outdated security practices and accessibility issues propagated through reuse. Our findings inform tool designers, app store policies, and researchers aiming to foster innovative yet safe UI development.},
