@inproceedings{boonprakong_workshop_2023,
 abstract = {Visual programming languages (VPLs) have long been proposed as a more accessible alternative to textual programming languages (TPLs), especially for novice programmers. However, VPLs have not seen widespread adoption, in part due to their limited expressiveness and the difficulty of scaling them to complex tasks. In this paper, we explore the design space of VPLs for interactive data processing, a domain where VPLs have shown promise but where challenges remain. We present a taxonomy of VPL design dimensions, including representation, interaction, and computation models, and use it to analyze existing VPLs. We then introduce DataFlow, a novel VPL that combines a node-link diagram representation with direct manipulation interactions and a dataflow computation model. DataFlow supports a wide range of data processing tasks, from simple filtering and aggregation to complex machine learning pipelines, while remaining accessible to novices. We evaluate DataFlow through a user study with 24 participants, comparing it to a TPL (Python with Pandas) and another VPL (Node-RED). Our results show that DataFlow enables faster task completion and lower error rates for novices, while maintaining performance comparable to TPLs for experts. We discuss implications for VPL design and future research directions.},
