@article{bostanci_chatgpt_2025,
 abstract = {### Background
Robot-assisted surgery has gained popularity in liver surgery, but its role in colorectal liver metastases (CRLM) remains unclear. This study aimed to compare the perioperative outcomes of robot-assisted liver resection (RALR) versus laparoscopic liver resection (LLR) for CRLM.

### Methods
A systematic search of PubMed, Embase, and Cochrane databases was conducted up to December 2023. Studies comparing RALR and LLR for CRLM were included. The primary outcome was blood loss. Secondary outcomes included operative time, transfusion rate, length of stay, R0 resection rate, postoperative complications, and mortality. Random-effects meta-analysis was performed.

### Results
Seven studies with 1,098 patients (RALR: 388; LLR: 710) were included. RALR was associated with less blood loss (mean difference [MD] -71.5 mL, 95% CI -128.3 to -14.7, p = 0.01), shorter length of stay (MD -0.7 days, 95% CI -1.3 to -0.1, p = 0.03), and lower transfusion rate (odds ratio [OR] 0.47, 95% CI 0.25-0.89, p = 0.02). Operative time was longer in RALR (MD 36.5 min, 95% CI 10.5-62.5, p = 0.006). No differences were found in complications (OR 0.88, 95% CI 0.60-1.30, p = 0.53), mortality (OR 0.72, 95% CI 0.20-2.63, p = 0.62), or R0 resection rate (OR 1.07, 95% CI 0.69-1.66, p = 0.77).

### Conclusions
RALR for CRLM is associated with reduced blood loss, shorter hospital stay, and lower transfusion rates compared to LLR, despite longer operative times. Both approaches are safe with comparable oncologic outcomes.},
