 abstract = {This paper proposes a lightweight federated learning framework for anomaly detection in resource-constrained IoT devices. Traditional centralized machine learning approaches for IoT security face challenges such as data privacy concerns, high communication overhead, and vulnerability to single-point failures. To address these issues, we introduce a novel privacy-preserving model that enables collaborative training across distributed edge devices without sharing raw data. Our approach leverages a customized convolutional neural network (CNN) architecture optimized for low-power microcontrollers, combined with differential privacy mechanisms to safeguard sensitive information. Experimental evaluations on benchmark IoT datasets, including NSL-KDD and CIC-IDS2017, demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves a detection accuracy of 98.2% while reducing communication costs by 75% compared to baseline methods. Furthermore, the model exhibits robustness against adversarial attacks, making it suitable for real-world deployment in smart home and industrial IoT applications.},
