@inproceedings{danuarta_retrieval-augmented_2024,
 abstract = {Machine Learning-Based Prediction of Student Performance in Online Learning Environments

Online learning has become increasingly prevalent, especially following the global shift to remote education. Predicting student performance in these environments is crucial for timely interventions to improve outcomes. This paper proposes a machine learning framework to predict student performance using data from learning management systems (LMS). We employ a combination of demographic, behavioral, and interaction features extracted from LMS logs. Four supervised learning algorithms—Random Forest, Support Vector Machine, Gradient Boosting, and Neural Networks—are evaluated on a dataset of 1,000 students from a university online course. The Random Forest model achieves the highest accuracy of 92.5% in classifying student performance levels (high, medium, low). Feature importance analysis reveals that engagement metrics, such as time spent on modules and quiz scores, are the most predictive. The framework demonstrates robustness across different course types and suggests practical implications for educators to personalize learning support.},
