@article{el-bassel_practical_2025,
 abstract = {Background

Digital health interventions (DHIs) have the potential to improve access to smoking cessation support, but their implementation in routine care remains limited. This study aimed to explore the implementation of a DHI for smoking cessation (Quit Sense) in routine primary care in England.

Methods

We conducted a qualitative process evaluation alongside a cluster randomised controlled trial (RCT) of Quit Sense in routine primary care. We purposively sampled general practices from the intervention arm of the RCT and conducted semi-structured interviews with healthcare professionals (HCPs) and patients who had been offered Quit Sense. Data were analysed thematically.

Results

We interviewed 20 HCPs (GPs, nurses, health care assistants) from 13 practices and 15 patients. HCPs valued the potential of Quit Sense to support patients who were not ready to quit or engage with existing services. However, implementation was limited by a lack of integration with existing systems and workflows, and concerns about the digital divide. Patients appreciated the personalised support but some found the app complex. HCPs suggested that training and integration with electronic health records would facilitate implementation.

Conclusions

Implementation of DHIs in routine primary care requires consideration of system-level factors, including integration with existing workflows and addressing inequities in access. Future implementation efforts should involve co-design with HCPs and patients to ensure usability and equity.

Trial registration

