 abstract = {Image-based rendering (IBR) methods have achieved remarkable success in novel view synthesis. However, they often fail to preserve the appearance of the reference images when the novel view deviates significantly from the reference views. This is because existing IBR methods typically warp pixels from the reference images to the novel view without considering the complex light transport. In this paper, we propose a novel neural IBR method that preserves the appearance of the reference images while synthesizing photorealistic novel views. Our key insight is that the appearance of the reference images can be preserved by explicitly modeling the light transport in the reference images using a neural field. Specifically, we represent the scene as a neural light field that encodes the light transport in the reference images. To synthesize a novel view, we first warp the pixels from the reference images to the novel view using the neural light field, and then refine the warped pixels using a neural renderer. We demonstrate that our method outperforms state-of-the-art IBR methods in terms of both appearance preservation and novel view quality.},
