@inproceedings{golgoon_mechanistic_2024,
 abstract = {Interactive data visualization tools empower users to explore and analyze data through direct manipulation interfaces. However, creating custom visualizations often requires programming expertise, limiting accessibility. Visual programming environments aim to bridge this gap by allowing users to build visualizations using graphical blocks instead of code. Despite their promise, existing visual programming tools for data visualization face challenges in expressiveness, usability, and integration with data processing pipelines. In this paper, we explore the design space of visual programming for interactive data visualization through the development and evaluation of VizFlow, a novel system that enables users to create complex, interactive visualizations using a block-based interface. VizFlow supports a wide range of visualization types, including charts, maps, and networks, and integrates data transformation operations seamlessly. We conducted a user study with 24 participants, including novice and expert users, to assess VizFlow's usability and effectiveness compared to traditional coding and other visual tools. Results show that VizFlow significantly reduces development time for novices while maintaining expressiveness for experts, highlighting key design principles for future visual programming systems in data visualization.},
