@inproceedings{gu_lens_2024,
 abstract = {Nanoparticles have been widely used in various biomedical applications due to their unique properties. However, their potential toxicity and environmental impact remain a concern. In this study, we investigated the ecotoxicological effects of silver nanoparticles (AgNPs) on the freshwater algae Chlorella vulgaris. AgNPs were synthesized using a green method with plant extracts and characterized by UV-Vis spectroscopy, dynamic light scattering (DLS), and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The algae were exposed to different concentrations of AgNPs (0.1-10 mg/L) for 96 hours, and growth inhibition, chlorophyll content, oxidative stress markers, and ultrastructural changes were assessed. Results showed a concentration-dependent inhibition of algal growth, with an EC50 value of 2.5 mg/L. Chlorophyll a and b contents decreased significantly, while reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels and malondialdehyde (MDA) content increased, indicating oxidative damage. TEM analysis revealed AgNP internalization and disruption of chloroplast structure. These findings highlight the potential risks of AgNPs to aquatic ecosystems and underscore the need for further research on their safe application.},
