 abstract = {The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence (AI) technologies has significantly impacted various sectors, including healthcare, where AI-driven diagnostic tools are increasingly integrated into clinical workflows. This paper presents a novel framework for AI-assisted medical image analysis, focusing on the detection and classification of anomalies in chest X-rays for early diagnosis of respiratory diseases. Our approach leverages a hybrid deep learning model combining convolutional neural networks (CNNs) with transformer architectures to enhance feature extraction and contextual understanding. The model was trained on a large-scale dataset comprising over 100,000 annotated images from public repositories such as ChestX-ray14 and MIMIC-CXR. Experimental results demonstrate superior performance, achieving an accuracy of 96.2%, precision of 95.8%, and recall of 94.7%, outperforming state-of-the-art methods like ResNet-50 and DenseNet-121. Furthermore, we address ethical considerations, including bias mitigation and interpretability through attention mechanisms and Grad-CAM visualizations. This framework not only improves diagnostic efficiency but also supports clinicians in resource-limited settings, paving the way for more accessible and reliable AI applications in global health.},
