@article{imundo_expert_2024,
 abstract = {Identity is a central component of the self-concept and is associated with numerous psychological outcomes. Military service is a profound experience that likely influences identity, yet no psychometrically sound measure of military identity exists. The present investigation details the development and initial validation of the Military Identity Scale (MIS), a brief self-report measure of military identity. In Study 1 (N = 1,023), exploratory and confirmatory factor analyses supported a single-factor structure for the 5-item MIS with strong internal consistency (α = .95). In Study 2 (N = 306), the MIS demonstrated expected relations with measures of military experience and other identity domains, providing evidence of convergent and discriminant validity. Test–retest reliability over 2 weeks was high (r = .89). The MIS is a reliable and valid measure of military identity that can be used in future research to better understand the role of military identity in veterans’ adjustment.},
