 abstract = {We present Haptics4All, a framework for designing inclusive haptic experiences for people with upper-body mobility impairments. Through a series of workshops with 12 participants with varying impairments, we identified key challenges in current wearable haptic designs, including accessibility barriers, customization needs, and social acceptability. Our framework integrates modular hardware components, adaptive software algorithms, and user-centered co-design principles to enable personalized haptic feedback. We evaluate Haptics4All through a user study with 15 participants, demonstrating significant improvements in usability (p<0.01) and satisfaction compared to baseline systems. Our work contributes to more equitable haptic technologies, with implications for rehabilitation, gaming, and virtual reality applications.},
