 abstract = {Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have led to their widespread adoption in various applications. However, the black-box nature of these models poses challenges in understanding and trusting their outputs, particularly in high-stakes domains. Post-hoc interpretability methods aim to explain model decisions after training, but they often struggle with the complexity and scale of LLMs. In this paper, we propose a novel approach to enhance the interpretability of LLMs by integrating mechanistic interpretability techniques with visualization tools. Our method decomposes the model's internal computations into interpretable circuits, revealing how specific input features influence the output. We evaluate our approach on benchmark tasks and demonstrate its effectiveness in providing human-understandable explanations. Our findings suggest that this hybrid method can bridge the gap between model performance and user trust, paving the way for more transparent AI systems.},
