@inproceedings{kong_document_2024,
 abstract = {Machine Learning-Based Prediction of Drug Response in Cancer Cell Lines Using Multi-Omics Data Integration

Cancer is a leading cause of death worldwide, and personalized medicine aims to tailor treatments to individual patients based on their tumor's molecular profile. However, predicting drug response remains challenging due to the complexity of cancer biology. In this study, we developed a machine learning framework that integrates multi-omics data to predict drug response in cancer cell lines. We utilized gene expression, DNA methylation, and copy number variation data from the Genomics of Drug Sensitivity in Cancer (GDSC) database. Our approach employed a deep neural network model, which outperformed traditional methods like elastic net regression in terms of prediction accuracy. The model achieved an average Pearson correlation coefficient of 0.72 across 138 drugs tested on 265 cell lines. Feature importance analysis revealed key genes and pathways associated with drug sensitivity, providing insights into potential therapeutic targets. This framework holds promise for advancing precision oncology by enabling more accurate predictions of patient responses to targeted therapies.},
