 abstract = {This paper explores the use of large language models (LLMs) in supporting creative writing tasks. Through a mixed-methods approach involving surveys, interviews, and workshop sessions with 45 participants, we investigate how writers integrate LLMs into their workflows for ideation, drafting, and revision. Our findings reveal that while LLMs enhance productivity and spark novel ideas, users often face challenges related to originality, coherence, and ethical concerns such as authorship attribution. We discuss design implications for LLM interfaces that better align with creative practices, including adaptive prompting and collaborative editing features. This work contributes to the growing body of research on human-AI co-creation in artistic domains.},
