@inproceedings{li_towards_2024,
 abstract = {Bystanders are a critical yet understudied aspect of public displays. While prior work has explored how public displays affect passersby, little is known about how they affect people who intentionally stop to interact with the display. We conducted a study in-the-wild with 40 participants to investigate how bystanders affect people interacting with public displays. We found that interactors are highly aware of bystanders and adjust their behavior accordingly, often to avoid embarrassment or social awkwardness. We also found that bystanders can have a positive effect on interactors, such as providing social proof or encouragement. Our findings suggest that designers of public displays should consider the social dynamics between interactors and bystanders when designing interactions.},
