@article{liu_backdoor_2025,
 abstract = {Federated learning (FL) has emerged as a promising paradigm for collaborative model training across distributed devices while preserving data privacy. However, the advent of large models, such as large language models (LLMs), introduces new challenges to FL, including escalated communication costs, intensified privacy risks, and the necessity for efficient personalization. This survey provides a comprehensive overview of FL in the context of large models, delving into its core methodologies, pivotal applications, and prospective research directions. We first delineate the unique challenges posed by large models in FL settings. Subsequently, we systematically review the state-of-the-art techniques addressing these challenges, encompassing communication-efficient algorithms, privacy-preserving mechanisms, and personalization strategies. Furthermore, we explore diverse applications of large model FL across domains such as natural language processing, computer vision, and recommender systems. Finally, we discuss open issues and future research avenues to advance the field.},
