@article{liu_review_2025,
 abstract = {Federated learning (FL) has emerged as a promising paradigm for collaborative machine learning across distributed devices while preserving data privacy by keeping raw data localized. Despite its advantages, FL faces several challenges, particularly in resource-constrained environments like the Internet of Things (IoT) and edge computing. This paper provides a comprehensive survey on FL, focusing on its applications, techniques, challenges, and future directions in IoT and edge computing contexts. We first introduce the fundamentals of FL, including its architecture, communication protocols, and optimization strategies. Then, we categorize existing FL approaches based on model aggregation methods, handling data heterogeneity, and privacy-preserving mechanisms. Key challenges such as communication overhead, computational limitations, non-IID data distribution, and security vulnerabilities are discussed in detail, along with state-of-the-art solutions like compression techniques, differential privacy, and secure multi-party computation. We also review real-world applications of FL in IoT domains, including smart healthcare, intelligent transportation, and industrial automation. Finally, we outline open research issues and potential future directions, such as integration with blockchain for enhanced security and scalability in 6G networks.},
