@inproceedings{margabandu_design_2025,
 abstract = {Mutation Testing for Deep Learning Systems: A Systematic Mapping Study

Deep learning (DL) systems have been widely applied in safety-critical domains, such as autonomous driving and medical diagnosis. However, DL systems are vulnerable to adversarial attacks and suffer from poor interpretability, which poses significant challenges to their reliability and trustworthiness. Mutation testing is a well-established fault-based testing technique that has been adapted to DL systems to evaluate the effectiveness of test cases. Despite the growing interest in mutation testing for DL systems, there is a lack of comprehensive understanding of the state-of-the-art. In this paper, we conduct a systematic mapping study to synthesize the existing research on mutation testing for DL systems. We identify 52 primary studies from 2017 to 2023 and classify them based on research types, mutation operators, application domains, and evaluation metrics. Our findings reveal that most studies focus on proposing new mutation operators tailored to DL models, with a emphasis on convolutional neural networks (CNNs). We also highlight open challenges and future directions, including the need for standardized benchmarks and empirical evaluations across diverse DL architectures. This study provides researchers and practitioners with a structured overview to guide future advancements in testing DL systems.},
