@article{mcisaac_large_2025,
 abstract = {Purpose: This study aimed to evaluate the efficacy of dexmedetomidine as an adjuvant to ropivacaine in ultrasound-guided adductor canal block for postoperative analgesia in patients undergoing total knee arthroplasty.

Methods: Eighty patients scheduled for total knee arthroplasty under spinal anesthesia were randomly assigned to receive either 0.5% ropivacaine 20 mL with dexmedetomidine 1 μg/kg (group RD, n = 40) or 0.5% ropivacaine 20 mL alone (group R, n = 40) for adductor canal block. The primary outcome was the duration of sensory block. Secondary outcomes included postoperative pain scores, time to first analgesic request, total analgesic consumption, and side effects.

Results: The duration of sensory block was significantly longer in group RD than in group R (median [interquartile range], 12 [10–14] hr vs 8 [7–9] hr; P < 0.001). Postoperative visual analogue scale scores were lower in group RD at 6, 12, and 24 hr after surgery (P < 0.05). The time to first analgesic request was longer in group RD (median [interquartile range], 14 [12–16] hr vs 9 [8–10] hr; P < 0.001), and sufentanil consumption in the first 24 hr was lower in group RD (mean [standard deviation], 25 [5] μg vs 35 [6] μg; P < 0.001). No significant differences were found in the incidence of side effects between the two groups.

Conclusion: Adding dexmedetomidine to ropivacaine in ultrasound-guided adductor canal block prolongs sensory block duration and improves postoperative analgesia without increasing side effects in patients undergoing total knee arthroplasty.},
