@article{nam_multimodal_2025,
 abstract = {Purpose: To investigate the diagnostic performance of a deep learning (DL)-based algorithm for detecting thyroid nodules on ultrasound (US) images and to compare it with that of radiologists.

Materials and Methods: This retrospective study included 1,024 US images of thyroid nodules from 512 patients (mean age, 48.5 ± 12.3 years; 412 women) who underwent US-guided fine-needle aspiration or biopsy between January 2019 and December 2020. A DL model was trained using 70% of the dataset and tested on the remaining 30%. The diagnostic performance of the DL model was compared with that of three radiologists with varying levels of experience (junior, intermediate, and senior) using the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC), sensitivity, specificity, and accuracy. McNemar’s test was used to compare the diagnostic performance between the DL model and radiologists.

Results: The DL model showed an AUC of 0.912 (95% confidence interval [CI], 0.885–0.934) for detecting malignant thyroid nodules, which was comparable to those of the junior (0.875; 95% CI, 0.845–0.902; p = 0.124), intermediate (0.896; 95% CI, 0.869–0.920; p = 0.456), and senior radiologists (0.934; 95% CI, 0.912–0.952; p = 0.678). The sensitivity, specificity, and accuracy of the DL model were 89.2% (95% CI, 84.5–93.1), 85.6% (95% CI, 80.2–90.1), and 87.5% (95% CI, 83.2–91.2), respectively, which were not significantly different from those of the radiologists (all p > 0.05).

Conclusion: The DL-based algorithm demonstrated diagnostic performance comparable to that of radiologists for detecting thyroid nodules on US images, suggesting its potential as an assistive tool in clinical practice.},
