 abstract = {Federated Learning (FL) enables collaborative model training across distributed devices while keeping data local, enhancing privacy. However, FL is vulnerable to inference attacks that reconstruct private training data. Existing defenses, such as differential privacy (DP), often degrade model utility. In this work, we propose a novel FL framework, DP-FedAvg, that integrates DP noise into the federated averaging process to provide rigorous privacy guarantees without significantly compromising accuracy. Our approach perturbs model updates during aggregation, ensuring that the released model does not reveal sensitive information about individual clients' data. Extensive experiments on benchmark datasets demonstrate that DP-FedAvg achieves strong privacy-utility trade-offs, outperforming baseline DP methods in FL settings.},
