@book{noauthor_ares_2024,
 abstract = {Alzheimer's disease (AD) is the most common form of dementia and is characterized by progressive cognitive decline and neurodegeneration. The pathological hallmarks of AD include extracellular amyloid-β (Aβ) plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles composed of hyperphosphorylated tau protein. Despite extensive research, the exact mechanisms underlying AD pathogenesis remain unclear, and effective disease-modifying treatments are lacking. Recent evidence suggests that neuroinflammation plays a crucial role in AD progression. Microglia, the resident immune cells of the central nervous system, are key mediators of neuroinflammation in AD. In the healthy brain, microglia perform essential functions including synaptic pruning, debris clearance, and immune surveillance. However, in AD, microglia become chronically activated and can adopt both neuroprotective and neurotoxic phenotypes. This review discusses the dual role of microglia in AD, highlighting how microglial dysfunction contributes to disease pathology. We examine the molecular mechanisms regulating microglial activation states, including genetic risk factors such as TREM2 and CD33, and explore how microglia interact with Aβ plaques and tau pathology. Furthermore, we discuss emerging therapeutic strategies targeting microglial function as potential disease-modifying approaches for AD. Understanding the complex role of microglia in AD may provide new insights into disease mechanisms and reveal novel therapeutic targets for this devastating neurodegenerative disorder.},
