@book{noauthor_aspdac_2024,
 abstract = {Obesity is a major risk factor for type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and certain types of cancer. The global obesity epidemic has been attributed to increased caloric intake and decreased physical activity. However, emerging evidence suggests that environmental chemicals may also contribute to obesity by disrupting metabolic processes. These chemicals, termed "obesogens," can promote adipogenesis, alter metabolic setpoints, and disrupt energy balance and appetite controls. Obesogens include compounds such as tributyltin, bisphenol A, phthalates, and persistent organic pollutants. Exposure during critical developmental windows may be particularly important, as it can lead to persistent changes in metabolic programming. This review examines the evidence for obesogen effects, discusses potential mechanisms of action, and considers the implications for obesity prevention and treatment. Understanding the role of environmental chemicals in obesity may lead to new strategies for addressing the obesity epidemic.},
