@book{noauthor_asplos_2024,
 abstract = {Obesity is a major risk factor for type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and certain types of cancer. The mechanisms underlying obesity-associated metabolic dysfunction remain incompletely understood. Here, we show that obesity promotes the accumulation of senescent cells in adipose tissue and that clearance of senescent cells alleviates obesity-induced metabolic dysfunction. We found that high-fat diet (HFD)-induced obesity in mice increases the abundance of senescent cells, particularly senescent preadipocytes, in visceral adipose tissue. Genetic clearance of senescent cells using INK-ATTAC mice or pharmacological elimination of senescent cells using senolytic drugs (dasatinib plus quercetin) reduced adipose tissue macrophage infiltration and inflammation and improved metabolic parameters, including glucose tolerance and insulin sensitivity, in obese mice. Furthermore, transplantation of senescent preadipocytes into lean mice was sufficient to induce adipose tissue inflammation and systemic insulin resistance. These findings demonstrate that cellular senescence contributes to obesity-associated metabolic dysfunction and suggest that targeting senescent cells may represent a therapeutic strategy for obesity-related metabolic diseases.},
