 abstract = {Obesity is a major risk factor for type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and certain types of cancer. The mechanisms underlying obesity-associated metabolic dysfunction remain incompletely understood. Here, we show that obesity promotes the accumulation of senescent cells in adipose tissue and that clearance of senescent cells alleviates obesity-induced metabolic dysfunction. We found that high-fat diet (HFD)-induced obesity in mice increases the abundance of senescent cells, particularly senescent preadipocytes, in adipose tissue. Genetic clearance of senescent cells using INK-ATTAC mice or pharmacological elimination of senescent cells using senolytic drugs (dasatinib plus quercetin) reduced adipose tissue macrophage infiltration and crown-like structures, improved glucose tolerance and insulin sensitivity, and reduced adipose tissue inflammation in obese mice. Mechanistically, senescent preadipocytes exhibited a senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP) that promoted macrophage chemotaxis and adipose tissue inflammation. These findings demonstrate that cellular senescence contributes to obesity-associated metabolic dysfunction and suggest that targeting senescent cells may represent a therapeutic strategy for obesity-related metabolic diseases.},
