@book{noauthor_campc_2023,
 abstract = {Obesity is a major risk factor for type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and certain types of cancer. The prevalence of obesity has increased dramatically over the past few decades, and this trend shows no signs of abating. Although genetic factors play a role in determining susceptibility to obesity, the rapid increase in obesity prevalence is likely due to changes in environmental factors, particularly increased availability of energy-dense foods and decreased physical activity. The adipose tissue is not merely a passive reservoir for energy storage but is an active endocrine organ that secretes a variety of hormones and cytokines, collectively termed adipokines, which play important roles in regulating energy homeostasis, insulin sensitivity, and inflammation. Dysregulation of adipokine secretion in obesity contributes to the development of insulin resistance and other metabolic complications. Understanding the mechanisms by which adipose tissue dysfunction leads to metabolic disease may provide new therapeutic targets for the treatment of obesity and its associated complications.},
