@book{noauthor_chi_2023,
 abstract = {Alzheimer's disease (AD) is the most common form of dementia and is characterized by progressive cognitive decline and neurodegeneration. The pathological hallmarks of AD include extracellular amyloid-β (Aβ) plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles composed of hyperphosphorylated tau protein. Despite extensive research, the exact mechanisms underlying AD pathogenesis remain unclear, and effective disease-modifying treatments are lacking. Recent evidence suggests that neuroinflammation plays a crucial role in AD progression. Microglia, the resident immune cells of the central nervous system, are key mediators of neuroinflammation in AD. In the healthy brain, microglia perform essential functions including synaptic pruning, debris clearance, and immune surveillance. However, in AD, microglia become chronically activated and can adopt both beneficial and detrimental phenotypes. While microglia can clear Aβ deposits and support neuronal health, chronic activation leads to the release of pro-inflammatory cytokines, reactive oxygen species, and other neurotoxic factors that contribute to neuronal damage and disease progression. Understanding the complex and dynamic roles of microglia in AD is essential for developing therapeutic strategies that can modulate microglial function to slow or prevent disease progression. This review discusses the multifaceted roles of microglia in AD pathogenesis, including their involvement in Aβ clearance, tau pathology, synaptic loss, and neuroinflammation, and highlights potential therapeutic approaches targeting microglial function.},
