@book{noauthor_chi_2023-2,
 abstract = {Alzheimer's disease (AD) is the most common form of dementia and is characterized by progressive cognitive decline and neurodegeneration. The pathological hallmarks of AD include extracellular amyloid-β (Aβ) plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles composed of hyperphosphorylated tau protein. Recent evidence suggests that neuroinflammation plays a critical role in AD pathogenesis. Microglia, the resident immune cells of the central nervous system, are key mediators of neuroinflammation in AD. In the healthy brain, microglia perform essential functions including synaptic pruning, debris clearance, and immune surveillance. However, in AD, microglia become chronically activated and can adopt both beneficial and detrimental phenotypes. While microglia can phagocytose Aβ and promote its clearance, chronic activation leads to the release of pro-inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species that contribute to neuronal damage. Furthermore, genetic studies have identified several AD risk genes that are highly expressed in microglia, including TREM2, CD33, and CR1, highlighting the importance of microglial function in disease susceptibility. This review discusses the dual role of microglia in AD, examining how these cells transition from protective to pathological states and exploring potential therapeutic strategies targeting microglial function to modify disease progression.},
