 abstract = {Obesity is a major risk factor for type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and certain types of cancer. The global obesity epidemic has been attributed to increased caloric intake and decreased physical activity. However, emerging evidence suggests that environmental chemicals may also contribute to the obesity epidemic by disrupting metabolic processes. These chemicals, termed "obesogens," can promote adipogenesis and obesity by altering metabolic setpoints, disrupting appetite controls and satiety, and modifying energy balance to favor storage of calories. Obesogens may act during critical periods of development to alter the differentiation of adipocytes and the determination of metabolic setpoints that persist throughout life. This review examines the evidence for obesogen effects in experimental models and epidemiological studies, discusses potential mechanisms of action, and considers the implications for obesity prevention and treatment. Understanding the role of environmental chemicals in obesity may lead to new strategies for preventing and treating this major public health problem.},
