@book{noauthor_cscw_2023,
 abstract = {Obesity is a major risk factor for type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and certain types of cancer. The worldwide obesity epidemic has been attributed to increased caloric intake and decreased physical activity. However, emerging evidence suggests that exposure to environmental chemicals may also contribute to the obesity epidemic. Obesogens are defined as chemicals that inappropriately alter lipid homeostasis and fat storage, change metabolic setpoints, disrupt energy balance, or modify the regulation of appetite and satiety to promote fat accumulation and obesity. This review summarizes the evidence that exposure to environmental obesogens during critical periods of development can predispose exposed individuals to obesity later in life. We discuss the mechanisms by which obesogens act, including activation of nuclear receptors such as peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPARγ), retinoid X receptor (RXR), glucocorticoid receptor (GR), and estrogen receptor (ER), as well as other mechanisms. We also discuss the transgenerational effects of obesogen exposure and the potential for obesogens to interact with genetic susceptibility to obesity. Finally, we discuss the implications of the obesogen hypothesis for public health and suggest directions for future research.},
