@book{noauthor_cui_2023,
 abstract = {Obesity is a major risk factor for type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and certain cancers. The prevalence of obesity has increased dramatically over the past few decades, and this trend shows no signs of abating. Although genetic factors play a role in determining susceptibility to obesity, the rapid increase in obesity prevalence is likely due to environmental and behavioral factors. The gut microbiota has emerged as an important environmental factor that can influence energy balance and body weight. Studies in germ-free mice have shown that the gut microbiota can affect energy harvest from the diet and energy storage in adipose tissue. Furthermore, the composition of the gut microbiota differs between obese and lean individuals, and transfer of the gut microbiota from obese mice to germ-free mice can transmit the obese phenotype. These findings suggest that the gut microbiota may be a potential therapeutic target for obesity. However, the mechanisms by which the gut microbiota influences energy balance are not fully understood. This review summarizes current knowledge about the role of the gut microbiota in obesity and discusses potential mechanisms and therapeutic implications.},
