@article{noauthor_selected_2025,
 abstract = {Purpose To evaluate the performance of a novel deep learning-based framework for automated segmentation of brain tumors in multimodal MRI scans. Methods We propose a hybrid U-Net architecture incorporating attention mechanisms and multi-scale feature fusion, trained on the BraTS 2023 dataset comprising 1251 cases with T1, T1ce, T2, and FLAIR sequences. The model was validated using five-fold cross-validation and compared against state-of-the-art methods like nnU-Net and Swin-Unet. Results The proposed framework achieved Dice scores of 0.92, 0.89, and 0.87 for enhancing tumor, whole tumor, and tumor core regions, respectively, outperforming baselines by 3-5%. Sensitivity and specificity exceeded 0.90 across all classes. Inference time averaged 2.5 seconds per scan on a standard GPU. Conclusion Our approach demonstrates superior accuracy and efficiency for brain tumor segmentation, with potential for clinical integration to support radiologists in diagnosis and treatment planning.},
