@book{noauthor_umap_2023,
 abstract = {Alzheimer's disease (AD) is the most common form of dementia and is characterized by progressive cognitive decline and neurodegeneration. The pathological hallmarks of AD include extracellular amyloid-β (Aβ) plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles composed of hyperphosphorylated tau protein. Despite extensive research, the exact mechanisms underlying AD pathogenesis remain unclear, and effective disease-modifying treatments are lacking. Recent evidence suggests that neuroinflammation, mediated by activated microglia and astrocytes, plays a crucial role in AD progression. Microglia, the resident immune cells of the central nervous system, can adopt different activation states ranging from neuroprotective to neurotoxic phenotypes. In AD, chronic microglial activation contributes to synaptic loss, neuronal damage, and cognitive impairment. This review discusses the dual role of microglia in AD, highlighting how microglial dysfunction and chronic neuroinflammation contribute to disease pathology. We also examine potential therapeutic strategies targeting microglial activation and neuroinflammation as promising approaches for AD treatment. Understanding the complex interplay between neuroinflammation and AD pathogenesis may lead to the development of novel therapeutic interventions to slow or prevent disease progression.},
