@inproceedings{noh_biassist_2025,
 abstract = {Automated Fact-Checking of Claims in Narrative Texts

Narrative texts, such as news articles or encyclopedia entries, often contain factual claims that are not explicitly marked as such. Automated fact-checking systems typically operate on individual claims, ignoring the context in which they appear. We introduce a new task, narrative claim extraction, which consists of two subtasks: (1) extracting factual claims from narrative texts, and (2) predicting their veracity using evidence from the open web. We create a dataset for this task, consisting of 1,000 claims extracted from Wikipedia articles, annotated with veracity labels and supporting evidence. We propose a pipeline approach that first identifies factual claims using a sequence labeling model, then verifies them using a natural language inference model. Our experiments show that this approach achieves high precision in claim extraction and reasonable accuracy in veracity prediction, but struggles with claims that require world knowledge or multi-hop reasoning. We release our dataset and code to facilitate further research on this task.},
