@article{pelletier_evidence-based_2025,
 abstract = {Adipose-derived stem cells (ASCs) are mesenchymal stem cells that can be isolated from adipose tissue and have shown promise in regenerative medicine due to their multipotent differentiation potential and immunomodulatory properties. This protocol describes a method for isolating and culturing human ASCs from subcutaneous adipose tissue obtained via liposuction. The process involves enzymatic digestion of the adipose tissue to release the stromal vascular fraction (SVF), followed by centrifugation to separate the SVF pellet. The SVF is then plated in culture media to allow for the adherence and expansion of ASCs, which are plastic-adherent cells. The protocol includes detailed steps for tissue mincing, collagenase digestion, filtration, and differential centrifugation. Additionally, it covers the expansion of ASCs in culture, passaging, and characterization using flow cytometry for surface marker expression (CD73, CD90, CD105 positive; CD34, CD45 negative) and trilineage differentiation assays (adipogenic, osteogenic, chondrogenic). This method yields high-purity ASCs suitable for downstream applications in tissue engineering, cell therapy, and basic research. The entire process from tissue collection to initial plating can be completed within 4-6 hours, with subsequent culture expansion taking 1-2 weeks.},
