 abstract = {Reinforcement learning (RL) has achieved remarkable success in various domains, but its black-box nature poses challenges for trust and deployment in real-world applications. Explainable reinforcement learning (XRL) aims to address this by providing interpretable insights into RL agents' decision-making processes. This chapter surveys the landscape of XRL methods, categorizing them into three main groups: intrinsic methods that build interpretability into the RL model, post-hoc methods that explain trained models, and visualization techniques that offer intuitive representations of agent behavior. We discuss key challenges such as handling partial observability, multi-agent settings, and long-horizon tasks, and highlight evaluation metrics and benchmarks for XRL. Finally, we outline future directions, including integration with human-in-the-loop systems and standardized frameworks for XRL research.},
