@inproceedings{shanmugarasa_privacy_2025,
 abstract = {Haptic feedback is a promising modality for providing information to users of wearable devices, but designing effective haptic patterns for diverse user groups remains challenging. In this paper, we explore the design of haptic patterns for people with upper-body mobility impairments, a group often overlooked in haptic design research. Through a series of workshops and iterative design sessions with 12 participants with varying degrees of upper-body mobility, we identify key design considerations, including the need for customizable intensity and duration, integration with residual mobility, and multi-modal feedback to enhance accessibility. Our findings reveal that traditional haptic designs, which assume full mobility, can exclude users with impairments, leading to frustration and reduced usability. We propose a framework for inclusive haptic design that incorporates user-centered customization and testing with diverse abilities. This work contributes to more equitable wearable technology by highlighting the importance of inclusive practices in haptics research and development.},
