@article{shen_exploring_2025,
 abstract = {Background

Simulation-based education (SBE) is widely used in nursing education to enhance clinical skills and decision-making. However, its integration into nursing curricula varies, and there is a need to understand current practices and challenges.

Aim

This scoping review aimed to map the evidence on the integration of SBE in undergraduate nursing curricula, including facilitators, barriers, and outcomes.

Design

A scoping review following Arksey and O'Malley's framework and PRISMA-ScR guidelines.

Methods

Five databases (CINAHL, MEDLINE, Embase, Web of Science, ERIC) were searched from January 2013 to June 2023. Studies focusing on SBE integration in undergraduate nursing programs were included. Data were charted and analyzed thematically.

Results

Thirty-two studies were included. SBE integration ranged from standalone sessions to fully embedded curricula. Facilitators included institutional support, faculty development, and interprofessional collaboration. Barriers encompassed resource limitations, faculty resistance, and assessment challenges. Outcomes highlighted improved clinical competence, confidence, and knowledge retention, though evidence on long-term impacts was limited.

Conclusions

While SBE is increasingly integrated, standardized approaches and robust evaluation are needed to optimize its educational impact. Future research should explore sustainable integration models and equity in access.},
