@incollection{sutherland_fact_2025,
 abstract = {The rapid proliferation of online information has led to the spread of misinformation, including fake news, which poses significant threats to society, democracy, and individual well-being. Detecting fake news automatically is a challenging task due to its subjective nature and the lack of ground truth labels. In this paper, we present a comprehensive survey on fake news detection using machine learning and deep learning techniques. We categorize the existing approaches into feature-based methods, which rely on handcrafted features such as linguistic, structural, and propagation-based features, and end-to-end deep learning methods that learn representations directly from raw data. We discuss popular datasets, evaluation metrics, and the role of multimodal information in improving detection accuracy. Furthermore, we highlight the challenges in fake news detection, including adversarial attacks, domain adaptation, and explainability, and outline promising future research directions.},
