@article{swami_integration_2025,
 abstract = {Introduction: Risankizumab (RZB), a humanized immunoglobulin G1 monoclonal antibody that selectively binds with high affinity and specificity to interleukin-23, is approved for the treatment of moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis in adults who are candidates for systemic therapy or phototherapy. This multicenter, prospective, non-interventional, post-marketing observational study evaluated the real-world effectiveness and safety of RZB in Japanese patients with moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis over 2 years.

Methods: Patients aged ≥ 20 years with moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis who initiated RZB treatment were enrolled. The primary endpoint was the proportion of patients achieving a ≥ 90% improvement from baseline in Psoriasis Area and Severity Index (PASI 90) at week 16. Secondary endpoints included PASI 75, PASI 100, and absolute PASI scores at weeks 16, 52, and 104, as well as safety assessments.

Results: Of 200 enrolled patients, 150 (75.0%) completed 104 weeks of treatment. At week 16, 72.0% (144/200) of patients achieved PASI 90, 85.5% (171/200) achieved PASI 75, and 45.5% (91/200) achieved PASI 100. Response rates were maintained or improved through week 104 (PASI 90: 80.0% [120/150]; PASI 75: 90.7% [136/150]; PASI 100: 58.7% [88/150]). Mean ± standard deviation PASI scores decreased from 14.2 ± 8.5 at baseline to 1.2 ± 2.5 at week 104. Adverse events (AEs) were reported in 68.0% (136/200) of patients; most were mild (49.5%) or moderate (14.5%) in severity. Serious AEs occurred in 8.0% (16/200) of patients; none were considered related to RZB by investigators.

Conclusion: RZB demonstrated sustained effectiveness with a favorable safety profile over 2 years in real-world clinical practice in Japanese patients with moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis. These findings support the use of RZB as a long-term treatment option.},
