@article{tian_foundation_2024,
 abstract = {### Summary

The gut microbiota plays a critical role in human health, and disruptions in its composition have been linked to various diseases. Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) is a promising therapeutic approach for restoring microbial balance, particularly in conditions like Clostridioides difficile infection. However, the mechanisms underlying FMT efficacy remain poorly understood. Here, we investigate the short- and long-term effects of FMT on the gut microbiome and metabolome in patients with recurrent C. difficile infection using a multi-omics approach. We performed shotgun metagenomic sequencing, metabolomics, and metatranscriptomics on fecal samples collected before and after FMT. Our results reveal rapid engraftment of donor microbiota, with significant shifts in microbial diversity and functional pathways within days of treatment. Metabolomic profiling identified changes in bile acids, short-chain fatty acids, and secondary metabolites associated with successful engraftment. Longitudinal analysis showed sustained microbial stability over 6 months, correlating with clinical resolution. These findings provide insights into the dynamic interplay between microbiota, host metabolism, and FMT outcomes, informing future therapeutic strategies.},
