 abstract = {Phishing remains one of the most prevalent cyber threats, exploiting human vulnerabilities to deceive users into revealing sensitive information. Traditional phishing detection methods, such as rule-based filters and machine learning classifiers, often struggle with sophisticated attacks that mimic legitimate communications. This paper proposes a novel phishing detection framework that integrates Large Language Models (LLMs) with traditional features to enhance detection accuracy and explainability. We evaluate the performance of several LLMs, including GPT-3.5, GPT-4, and open-source models like LLaMA, on a comprehensive dataset comprising phishing and benign emails. Our results demonstrate that the LLM-augmented approach achieves a detection accuracy of 97.8%, surpassing conventional methods by 12%. Additionally, we introduce an explainability module using SHAP values to provide interpretable insights into model decisions, aiding in user education and system transparency. This framework offers a scalable solution for real-time phishing mitigation in email systems.},
