 abstract = {Conversational agents are increasingly integrated into everyday applications, yet their interactions often fail to meet user expectations, leading to frustration and disengagement. This paper investigates user expectations for conversational agents through a mixed-methods approach, combining surveys, interviews, and interaction logs from 500+ users across diverse applications (e.g., virtual assistants, customer service bots). We identify key expectation categories: response relevance, empathy, consistency, and proactivity. Findings reveal that unmet expectations correlate with 40% higher abandonment rates, with empathy and proactivity being the most critical gaps. We propose a framework for expectation-aware design, including adaptive response strategies and user feedback loops, validated through a prototype evaluation showing improved satisfaction scores by 25%. This work contributes to more human-centered conversational AI, emphasizing the need for dynamic expectation modeling in deployment.},
