 abstract = {Plant diseases cause significant economic losses in agriculture worldwide. Traditional disease detection methods are labor-intensive and time-consuming. Deep learning (DL) techniques have shown promising results in automated plant disease detection using image processing. This study reviews recent DL-based approaches for plant disease detection, focusing on convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and their variants. We categorize the methods based on data sources (leaf, fruit, stem), disease types, and performance metrics. Key datasets such as PlantVillage and PlantDoc are discussed, along with challenges like data scarcity, environmental variations, and model generalization. The review highlights hybrid models combining CNNs with transfer learning (e.g., VGGNet, ResNet, DenseNet) achieving accuracies over 95%. Future directions include edge computing integration and multi-modal data fusion for real-time field applications. This comprehensive analysis aids researchers in selecting suitable DL frameworks for robust plant disease diagnosis.},
