 abstract = {Current auditory interfaces for menu navigation often rely on speech output, which can be cognitively demanding and slow for frequent interactions. We introduce SonicMenu, a novel auditory menu system that uses non-speech sounds (earcons) combined with spatial audio to enable efficient, eyes-free menu selection. SonicMenu leverages distinct earcons for menu items, spatial positioning to indicate hierarchy, and gesture-based interaction for selection. Through a user study with 15 participants, we demonstrate that SonicMenu outperforms traditional speech-based menus in terms of selection speed (reduced by 40%) and error rate (lowered by 25%), while maintaining high user satisfaction. Our findings suggest that non-speech auditory cues can significantly enhance accessibility for visually impaired users in mobile contexts.},
