@article{zhu_explainability_2025,
 abstract = {Nanoscale engineering of 2D materials has enabled the fabrication of van der Waals heterostructures with atomically sharp interfaces and designer functionalities. However, the integration of 2D materials into functional devices often requires precise control over their electronic properties, which is challenging due to extrinsic factors such as defects and contamination. Here, we demonstrate a scalable approach to engineer the electronic properties of graphene–hBN heterostructures using a combination of plasma treatment and thermal annealing. By selectively functionalizing the graphene surface with oxygen plasma, we create a periodic array of defects that act as scattering centres, enabling tunable control over carrier mobility and density. Subsequent annealing in an inert atmosphere restores the pristine lattice while preserving the engineered disorder, resulting in devices with enhanced performance for radio-frequency applications. Our method offers a versatile platform for tailoring 2D heterostructures, paving the way for next-generation electronics and optoelectronics.},
